ce:section.ce:para:
The solubilization of WAS after pretreatment was evaluated using SCOD (Fig. 1
                        ). The values of SCOD in the tests of control, 0.1gCaO2/gVSS, and MW (480W, 2min) continued to increase within the first 4d, while the increasing trend stopped in 3d and 2d in the MW (480W, 2min)/CaO2 (0.1g/gVSS) and CaO2 (0.1g/gVSS)/MW (480W, 2min) groups, respectively. The maximum value of SCOD accounted for 18%, 32%, 35%, 52%, and 54% of the TCOD in the control, 0.1gCaO2/gVSS, MW (480W, 2min), MW (480W, 2min)/CaO2 (0.1g/gVSS), and CaO2 (0.1g/gVSS)/MW (480W, 2min) groups, respectively. These findings indicate that the combination of CaO2 and MW treatments can greatly enhance sludge disintegration and solubilization, and that adding CaO2 before MW irradiation has a stronger effect than after MW irradiation. Reductions of VSS and TS were also measured on day 16 (Table 2
                        ). The highest reductions of VSS (55.6%) and TS (37.6%) were observed in the CaO2 (0.1g/gVSS)/MW (480W, 2min) group, which was consistent with the solubilization of WAS (Fig. 1).
